# {{AGENT_NAME}} — Airtable Project Agent (operations)

Watches table `{{AIRTABLE_TABLE_ID}}` in base `{{AIRTABLE_BASE_ID}}` and
reacts to record activity. Event-driven — no cron, no servers to maintain.
Installed by [ai-automations](https://github.com/makasanaakshay/ai-automation-master);
re-run its installer to update (answers saved in `automation.config.json`).

```
Airtable webhook ping (HMAC-SHA256 verified via X-Airtable-Content-MAC)
  → Cloudflare Worker relay            scripts/airtable-agent/relay/worker.js
  → repository_dispatch                .github/workflows/airtable-agent.yml
  → agent-run.sh                       restore state → resolve → playbook → save state
```

| Trigger | Playbook | Output (one record comment) |
|---|---|---|
| Record enters **{{STATUS_ANALYZE}}** | `analyze` | Options + trade-offs + recommendation, or numbered questions |
| Human comment on a {{STATUS_ANALYZE}} record | `respond` | Answer / updated proposal / remaining questions |
| Record enters **{{STATUS_IMPLEMENT}}** (or comment there) | `implement` | PR into `{{PR_BASE}}` — or questions if unclear |

Airtable's webhook notifications are thin pings (`{base, webhook, timestamp}`,
no change details — details would need the payloads-cursor API). The doorbell
pattern collapses that two-step protocol into a ping: every verified ping
dispatches a reconcile run, and the scan (`filterByFormula` over both watched
options + tracked records) finds whatever changed — no cursor bookkeeping.
State lives on the orphan branch `airtable-agent-state`
(`state/<record_id>.json`: status_name, phase, last_comment_at, branch,
pr_url). Every run re-fetches truth from Airtable's API and diffs against it.
Agent comments are prefixed `{{AGENT_MARKER}} — `; the resolver skips marker
comments (loop protection). Comment recency is tracked by `createdTime`
(ISO-8601, string compare). `{{STATUS_FIELD}}` may be a single select (plain
string) or a status-type field (object with a name) — both are handled;
option matching is case-insensitive.

## Operating

- **Recover / backfill:** Actions → *Airtable Project Agent* → *Run workflow* — empty `item_id` reconciles both watched options + tracked records; a record id processes one.
- **Transcripts:** every run uploads `.agent-out/` as an artifact.
- **Model:** `AI_MODEL` in `scripts/airtable-agent/env.sh` (currently `{{AI_MODEL}}`, brain `{{BRAIN_NAME}}`).
- **Webhook expiry:** Airtable webhooks expire after **7 days** unless refreshed. Every agent run best-effort refreshes the webhook in `AIRTABLE_WEBHOOK_ID` (env.sh), so an active board keeps itself alive — but after 7+ quiet days the webhook lapses silently. Refresh it (`api.sh POST "/bases/{{AIRTABLE_BASE_ID}}/webhooks/<webhook_id>/refresh"`) or recreate it; list them with `api.sh GET "/bases/{{AIRTABLE_BASE_ID}}/webhooks"`.
- **Pause:** `api.sh DELETE "/bases/{{AIRTABLE_BASE_ID}}/webhooks/<webhook_id>"` (recreate to resume — new MAC secret, new id).
- **Known blind spot:** a dormant repo — no agent runs for 7+ days — wakes up deaf (expired webhook); poke it per the webhook-expiry bullet, then trigger a manual reconcile run.

## Local dry-run (safe — needs AIRTABLE_TOKEN exported)

```bash
DRY_RUN=1 bash scripts/airtable-agent/agent-run.sh                 # reconcile scan
DRY_RUN=1 ITEM_ID=<record_id> bash scripts/airtable-agent/agent-run.sh
```

## Guardrails

- Airtable writes are **comments only** — the agent never changes the status field, any other field, or anything else; humans own the board.
- PRs are **always against `{{PR_BASE}}`** (branch `{{BRANCH_PREFIX}}/<kebab-slug>`); the agent never merges and never pushes to `{{PR_BASE}}` directly.
- `--dangerously-skip-permissions` only inside the disposable CI runner; local runs are `DRY_RUN` and stop before any AI call.
